2:title: Restyle a caption track
9:There are two ways to restyle captions, depending on what you want to change. A word style and its looks are set when the captions are generated, so changing those means generating again. Font, outline and backdrop are ordinary text settings, which you can change on a run of captions in the inspector.
14:  tools={['transcribe', 'set_text']}
17:## Restyle by generating again
35:- **AssemblyAI:** the cached transcript is reused whatever style you ask for, so nothing is uploaded and nothing is billed. That holds while the language, **Speakers:** and **Vocabulary:** match the transcript and **Transcribe again:** stays unticked. See [Transcribe with AssemblyAI](/captions/assemblyai-transcription/).
36:- **On this Mac:** the audio is recognised again. It's free, but it takes the time again.
39:New pages are made from the transcript, not from the old track. Words you corrected, pages you split or trimmed, and anything you restyled by hand go with the old track. To page a track again and keep your corrections, use [Re-lay Captions](/captions/re-lay-captions/) instead.
50:- every one of those clips overlaps a clip being transcribed.
73:A change of **Font** on a word caption page, which a text preset also makes, means Re-lay Captions no longer recognises its track and [keeps it as placed](/captions/re-lay-captions/#tracks-kept-as-placed).
81:- If one file can't be transcribed, the captions from the other files are still placed and the old tracks still go, so the captions over that file's clips are lost. Undo brings them back.
87:`transcribe` restyles the same way, as long as it asks for a word style and places the captions. An agent can also change a placed page's word style directly with `set_text`, which keeps your corrected words. See [Word colours](/captions/word-caption-looks/#with-an-agent).

